    "conclusions": [
      "Block model loading and baking dominate reload Java execution samples. These counts are not elapsed-time percentages or predicted speedup.",
      "BakedModelCacheKey uses record equals/hashCode. Its hottest equality path is a normal cache lookup from weighted/multipart model baking; no recursive equality was found.",
      "Most HashMap TreeNode.find samples occur in a separate BakedModelManager blockstate-to-ModelIdentifier getOrDefault mapping. Tree-node stack depth is usually 1 or 2. No causal evidence of pathological BakedModelCacheKey hash collisions.",
      "AffineTransformation equality compares its matrix once. Hashing uses Objects.hash(matrix); changing caching assumptions about mutable transforms is not justified.",
      "FerriteCore quad dedup uses synchronized ObjectOpenCustomHashSet with per-int murmurHash3 hashing. Disabling it would trade away memory savings, so no disable recommendation.",
      "Raw/transformed game class dumps were unavailable. Vanilla intermediary bytecode and installed FerriteCore/ModernFix mixin bytecode were inspected; actual applied injection semantics are corroborated only by JFR stacks.",
      "A narrowly scoped explicit BakedModelCacheKey.equals preserving exact record equality and leaving hashCode/cache lifetime untouched is a possible experiment. Benchmark first; benefit is unproven and cannot be inferred as 8-9% elapsed-time savings."
    ],
    "notes": [
      "Bounds use second-resolution client log timestamps converted from KST to UTC; ends include the printed completion second. Approximate boundary uncertainty is up to one second. Probe duration is the precise wall-clock measurement.",
      "Java execution samples are statistical observations, not CPU seconds or elapsed-time percentages. Inclusive/category counts overlap and must not be added.",
      "Allocation weights are JFR sampled estimates, not live heap sizes or exact allocations. No user data, file I/O paths, environment, arguments, or process fields are exported."
    ]
